Subject: Interpreting empirical_rxx coefficients in a bifactor analysis
Message-ID: <murfaLTM3B0.Er4k67fYAwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/murfaLTM3B0/m/Er4k67fYAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I am in the process of doing a bifactor analysis of a school climate measure. My question has to do with how to interpret empirical_rxx coefficients for both the general and specific factors. I would like to use specific factors for further analyses (e.g., correlation with other scales) but the obtained empirical_rxx coefficients are low, <.60, leading me to be cautious in using them for additional analyses. Any guidance here would be most welcome.

From ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 31 13:46:07 2024
From: ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com (Phil Chalmers)
Date: Wed, 31 Jan 2024 13:46:07 +0000
Subject: Re: Interpreting empirical_rxx coefficients in a bifactor analysis
Message-ID: <murfaLTM3B0.1BzFfwDaAwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <murfaLTM3B0.Er4k67fYAwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <murfaLTM3B0.Er4k67fYAwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/murfaLTM3B0/m/1BzFfwDaAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The rxx estimates from a bifactor model would reflect McDonald's ωh estimates, which for the specific factors will likely be much lower than the general factor (by design). Had a unidimesional model been fitted that would have reflected McDonald's ω estimate instead. If the structure is better reflected by something like a correlated simple structure then you may have more luck with using the specific factors, though this depends entirely on whether such representation is a reasonable one given the intended test structure. HTH.

Phil

